{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","count":17,"questions":[{"id":51357,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The boiling point of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>001</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> aqueous solutions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math> should follows the order.","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi><mo><</mo><mi>NaCl</mi><mo><</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi><mo><</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi><mo><</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi>NaCl</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi><mo><</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo><</mo><mi>NaCl</mi><mo><</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Elevation in boiling point, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>ΔT</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub></math> is constant for a solvent)</p>\n\n<p>If molality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mfenced></math> is same, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>ΔT</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo></math> van't Hoff factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>)</mo></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math> only partially dissociates,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo><</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> almost completely dissociate to produce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> ions per formula respectively,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> respectively</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> The order of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo></math> the order of boiling points</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi><mo><</mo><mi>NaCl</mi><mo><</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo><</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6f6ee099d02c02e17de"},{"id":51358,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$18 \\mathrm{~g}$ of glucose $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6}\\right)$ dissolved in $1 \\mathrm{~kg}$ of water is heated to boiling. The boiling point (in $\\mathrm{K}$ ) measured at $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ pressure is closest to [Ebulioscopic constant, $K_{\\mathrm{b}}$ for water is $0.52 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. Consider absolute zero to be $\\left.-273.15^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}\\right]$","options":["$373.15$","$373.10$","$373.20$","$373.25$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} \\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{b}}=& \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}} \\times \\mathrm{m} \\\\=& 0.52 \\times \\frac{18 / 180}{1} \\\\ &=0.052 \\\\ \\therefore \\mathrm{B} \\cdot \\mathrm{P} &=373.15+0.052 \\\\ &=373.2 \\mathrm{~K} \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2135c571014ecbb1d17"},{"id":51359,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The variation of solubility of four different gases (G1, G2, etc.) in a given solvent with pressure at a constant temperature is shown in the plot.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=h