75</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Mass of solvent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>472</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p>\n<p>Molar mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>126</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Molality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>472</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>126</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mol</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi>kg</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>\n<p>Normality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>Number</mi><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><mi>equivalents</mi><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><mi>solute</mi><mo> </mo></mrow><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>Volume</mi><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><mi>solution</mi><mo> </mo><mfenced><mi>l</mi></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>Mass</mi><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><mi>solute</mi><mo> </mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced><mo>×</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mrow><mo> </mo><mi>Equivalent</mi><mo> </mo><mi>mass</mi><mo> </mo><mi>of</mi><mo> </mo><mi>solute</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo></math> Volume of solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>mL</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n<p>Equivalent mass of oxalic acid</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Molar</mi><mo>mass</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>63</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>equi</mi></math>.</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Normality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>63</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>19</mn></math> (equivalents)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></math></p>\n<p>or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>19</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da706611ff1ed3fdb0fee"},{"id":51362,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A mixture of toluene and benzene forms a nearly ideal solution. Assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>°</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>°</mo></math> to be the vapor pressures of pure benzene and toluene, respectively. The slope of the line obtained by plotting the total vapor pressure to the mole fraction of benzene is:-","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>°</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>°</mo></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>°</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>°</mo></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>°</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>°</mo></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mo>°</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>°</mo></mrow></mfenced><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mtext>total </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mfenced><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mfenced><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mfenced><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mfenced><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mfenced></math><br />\nComparing it with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>mx</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mtext>total </mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mfrac><munder><munder><mfenced><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>-</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfenced><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></munder></math>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da7486551077f25de77a2"},{"id":51363,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"By dissolving $0.35$ mole of sodium chloride in water, $1.30 \\mathrm{~L}$ of salt solution is obtained. The molarity of the resulting solution should be reported as -","options":["$0.3$","$0.269$","$0.27$","$0.3692$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\text { Molarity }=\\frac{\\text { Moles of Solute }}{\\text { Vol.of Solution }}=\\frac{0.35}{1.3}=0.269$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21302e220fb6e5b4b05"},{"id":51364,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$2.52 \\mathrm{~g}$ of oxalic acid dehydrate was disved in $100 \\mathrm{ml}$ of water, $10 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of this ution was diluted to 500 $\\mathrm{mL} .$ The normality of the final ution and the amount of oxalic acid $(\\mathrm{mg} / \\mathrm{mL}$ ) in the ution are respectively-","options":["$0.16 \\mathrm{~N}, 5.04$","$0.08 \\mathrm{~N}, 3.60$","$0.04 \\mathrm{~N}, 3.60$","$0.02 \\mathrm{~N}, 10.08$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Initial Normality\n\n\\mathrm{N}=\\frac{2.52 \\times 1000}{63 \\times 100}=0.4\n\n\\begin{array}{l}\n{ }_{\\mathrm{COOH}} \\\\\n\\therefore \\mathrm{N}_{1} \\mathrm{~V}_{1}=\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{~V}_{2} \\\\\n0.4 \\times 10=\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\times 500 \\\\\n\\mathrm{~N}_{2}=\\frac{0.4}{50}=0.08 \\mathrm{~N}\n\\end{array}\n\nThen final weight $\\quad N=\\frac{w \\times 1000}{E \\times V_{m}}$\n0.08=\\frac{w \\times 1000}{63 \\times 500}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213ce449a6a049d82c2"},{"id":51365,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A ution containing $8.0 \\mathrm{~g}$ of nicotine in $92 \\mathrm{~g}$ of water freezes $0.925$ degrees below the normal freezing point of water. If the molal freezing point depression constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}=-1.85^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ then the molar mass of nicotine is-","options":["16","80","320","160"],"answer":3,"solution":"No Solution","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2130bee95de281dd783","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51366,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A mixture of toluene and benzene boils at $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. Assuming ideal behaviour, the mole fraction of toluene in the mixture is closest to [Vapour pressures of pure toluene and pure benzene at $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ are $0.742$ and $1.800$ bar respectively. 1 atm $=1.013$ bar $]$","options":["$0.824$","$0.744$","$0.544$","$0.624$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$1.013=0.742 \\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{t}}+1.8 \\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{b}}1.013=0.742\\left(1-\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{b}}\\right)+1.8 \\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{b}}\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{b}}=0.256\\therefore \\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{t}}=1-\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{b}}=0.744$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213e3a09f2554c74b5a"},{"id":51367,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The values of the Henry's law constant of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ar</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> in water at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>67</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>41</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>34</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>86</mn><mo> </mo><mi>kbar</mi></math>, respectively. The order of their solubility in water at the same temperature and pressure is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ar</mi><mo>></mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>","<span style=\"color:null;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><mi>Ar</mi><mo>></mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></span>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><mi>Ar</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ar</mi><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>By Henry's law, </p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mrow><mi>gas</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mrow><mi>gas</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></msub></math> (in liquid solution)</p>\n\n<p>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mrow><mi>gas</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mo>×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mrow><mi>gas</mi><mo> </mo></mrow></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></math> Henry's law constant.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> At a given partial pressure of gas, </p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi>gas</mi></msub></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> The greater the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub></math>, the less its solubility.</p>\n\n<p>Here, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ar</mi><mo>></mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Solubility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>></mo><mi>Ar</mi></math>.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6f6850fafc6d65adf60"},{"id":51368,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The density of acetic acid vapor at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ and $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ is $5 \\mathrm{mg} \\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$. The number of acetic acid molecules in the cluster that is formed in the gas phase is closest to","options":["5","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"solution":"$d=\\frac{P M}{R T} \\quad M=\\frac{d R T}{P}M=123\\therefore$ acetic acid exist as dimer","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2138f486a381bb74963"},{"id":51369,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The weight percent of sucrose (formula weight $\\left.=342 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$ in an aqueous solution is $3.42$. The density of the solution is $1 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mL}^{-1}$, the concentration of sucrose in the solution in $\\mathrm{mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$ is","options":["$0.01$","$0.1$","$1.0$","$10$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$3.42 \\mathrm{~gm}$ sacrose in $100 \\mathrm{~gm}$ solution\n$\\mathrm{d}=1 \\mathrm{~gm} \\mathrm{~ml}^{-1}\\because \\mathrm{d}=\\frac{\\text { mass }}{\\text { volume }}$\nvolume of solution $=\\frac{100}{1}=100 \\mathrm{~ml}$\nMolarity $=\\frac{\\mathrm{n}}{\\mathrm{v}} \\times 1000$\nMolarity $=\\frac{3.42}{342 \\times 100} \\times 1000=0.1$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213b1e78d8a59b31ca4"},{"id":51370,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In $108 \\mathrm{~g}$ of water, $18 \\mathrm{~g}$ of a non-volatile compound is dissolved. At $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ the vapor pressure of the solution is $750 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$. Assuming that the compound does not undergo association or dissociation, the molar mass of the compound in $\\mathrm{g} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ is","options":["128","182","152","228"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\frac{\\mathrm{n}}{\\mathrm{N}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{O}}-\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{S}}}{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{O}}}\\frac{18}{\\mathrm{~m}} \\times \\frac{18}{108}=\\frac{760-750}{760}\\frac{18}{6 \\mathrm{~m}}=\\frac{10}{760}6 \\mathrm{~m}=18 \\times 76\\mathrm{~m}=228$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213cc51d1c77f95716b"},{"id":51371,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The plot of total vapour pressure as a function of mole fraction of the components of an ideal solution formed by mixing liquids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math> is","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F44bcf377-84d7-4082-a9b5-55b60f858480-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F44bcf377-84d7-4082-a9b5-55b60f858480-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F44bcf377-84d7-4082-a9b5-55b60f858480-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fc6489965-5aa6-44fb-a5f0-02eea24084a9-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc6489965-5aa6-44fb-a5f0-02eea24084a9-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc6489965-5aa6-44fb-a5f0-02eea24084a9-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Faa90cd3b-3927-4037-9295-77eac8328673-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Faa90cd3b-3927-4037-9295-77eac8328673-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Faa90cd3b-3927-4037-9295-77eac8328673-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fe2751183-d839-43c2-8182-7e2940caf1ae-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fe2751183-d839-43c2-8182-7e2940caf1ae-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fe2751183-d839-43c2-8182-7e2940caf1ae-image.png?alt=media\"/>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>According to Raoult's law,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>According to Dalton's law of partial pressure,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mi>xχ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mi>yχ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mi>xχ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></msub></math></p>\n\n<p>Thus, total vapour pressure of solution varies linearly with mole fraction of component <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>, at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">χ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>, at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da68f6b9e574bba906e9e"},{"id":51372,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Solutions","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The solubility curve of $\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ in water is shown below.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FyY7QQqN14g_gCtVdZ-Lnn7DVq7hM6A_Sx7-5HA3U0WE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FyY7QQqN14g_gCtVdZ-Lnn7DVq7hM6A_Sx7-5HA3U0WE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FyY7QQqN14g_gCtVdZ-Lnn